<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basques</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Basques are a people of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, with a distinct language unrelated to other European languages. In 778 Basque forces ambushed the rearguard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-roncevaux-pass">Roncevaux</a> Pass. The episode, later celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Roland, was a notable setback for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basques.jpg" alt="Basques" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniele Schirmo aka Frankie688 · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Basque_Country.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Battle of Roncevaux Pass (778), in which Charlemagne's rearguard under the margrave Roland was ambushed and destroyed by <b>Basques</b> during the withdrawal from an unsuccessful Spanish campaign, showed that the Carolingian war machine had geographical limits.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 778 Charlemagne appointed a Frankish noble, Chorson, as count of Toulouse; after Chorson was captured by <b>Basques</b> and deposed in 790, the county passed to William of Gellone, a kinsman of the king, who defended the Spanish March against Umayyad raids and took part in the capture…</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 778 Charlemagne, invited by rebels in the north-east, marched on Zaragoza but had to withdraw, and his rearguard was destroyed by <b>Basques</b> at Roncesvalles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The population mixed Asturians, Cantabrians, Galicians, <b>Basques</b> and refugees of Hispano-Gothic descent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom of Pamplona, later called Navarre, arose among the <b>Basques</b> of the western Pyrenees, a people who had long resisted both the Visigoths and the Franks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Hispania the kings fought the Byzantines, who carved out the province of Spania during the civil war of 549–554, the Suebi, the <b>Basques</b>, Cantabrians and Asturians, and one another.</blockquote><a href="/empire/visigothic-kingdom">Visigothic Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
